    "answer": {
      "short": "$9990,\\ 4995$.",
      "worked": "Let the missing digits be $a$ and $b$, so $1\\le a\\le9$ and $0\\le b\\le9$. Divisibility by 5 requires $b=0$ or $b=5$. Divisibility by 9 requires $a+18+b$ to be a multiple of 9.\n\nIf $b=0$, the digit sum lies between 19 and 27, so its only possible multiple of 9 is 27:\n$$\\begin{align*}a+18+0&=27\\\\a&=9.\\end{align*}$$\nThis gives $9990$.\n\nIf $b=5$, the digit sum lies between 24 and 32, so its only possible multiple of 9 is 27:\n$$\\begin{align*}a+18+5&=27\\\\a&=4.\\end{align*}$$\nThis gives $4995$. Both numbers end in 0 or 5 and have digit sum 27, so both satisfy the required tests.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
